//! # Functions, generics, and errors
//!
//! Functions declare named parameters and an optional return type. A generic
//! parameter can carry trait bounds inline or in a `where` clause. Callable
//! values use `fn(T) -> U`; add `suspends` inside the brackets when a stored
//! callable may suspend, for example `fn[suspends](T) -> U`.
//!
//! ```hew
//! enum LookupError {
//!     Missing;
//! }
//!
//! fn identity<T>(value: T) -> T { value }
//!
//! fn require(value: Option<i64>) -> i64 fails LookupError {
//!     match value {
//!         .Some(number) => number,
//!         .None => return error .Missing,
//!     }
//! }
//!
//! fn next(value: Option<i64>) -> i64 fails LookupError {
//!     require(value)? + 1
//! }
//!
//! fn main() {
//!     match next(.Some(identity(41))) {
//!         .Ok(value) => println(value),
//!         .Err(_) => println("missing"),
//!     }
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ## Ownership parameters and receivers
//!
//! An ordinary parameter borrows or copies according to its type. Write
//! `consume value: T` when a call transfers an owned value. Method receivers
//! are `self`, `var self` when the method mutates a value receiver, or
//! `consume self` when the method terminates ownership. These modifiers are
//! part of the public API contract and appear in the generated reference.
//!
//! Closures use `|value: T| expression` or `|value: T| { ... }`. A stored
//! closure's callable type can also specify `var`, `once`, `clone`, and
//! `suspends` capabilities in brackets. The unqualified form is repeated and
//! read-only.
//!
//! ## Arguments and suspension
//!
//! Positional arguments come before named arguments; named arguments may then
//! appear in any order. Parameters with a default expression may be omitted.
//! Arguments evaluate left to right as written, followed by omitted defaults
//! in parameter order. Parameter names are therefore part of a public API.
//! Closures and values of function type accept positional arguments only,
//! because their types retain no parameter names or defaults.
//!
//! Hew has no `async fn` or `for await` syntax. Suspension is inferred from a
//! named function's body, and ordinary calls wait when needed. A stored
//! callable must state that possibility as `fn[suspends](T) -> U`; a callable
//! type without the qualifier promises not to suspend.
//!
//! ## Typed failure
//!
//! `fn operation() -> T fails E` has a success value `T` and a typed failure
//! edge `E`; calling it produces `Result<T, E>`. Leave through the failure edge
//! with `return error value` or `?` on a compatible `Result`. Handle a result
//! with `match`, `?`, or `handle error { ... }`. A dropped `Result` is a compile
//! error; write `let _ = operation();` only when discarding it is deliberate.
//!
//! `Option<T>` represents presence or absence. `?` on an `Option` propagates
//! absence only from a function returning `Option`; it does not invent an
//! error value. Use `expect(reason)` when absence or failure is an invariant
//! violation that should trap.
//!
//! ## Generators
//!
//! `gen fn values() -> T` declares `T` as the yielded item type. Calling it
//! returns a generator handle; writing `Generator<T, R>` as the declared
//! return type would instead mean that each yielded item is itself a generator
//! handle. A `for` loop drives a generator to completion. A failing generator
//! declared with `fails E` yields `Result<T, E>` items and stops after its
//! failure item. Generator parameters and captured owners follow the same
//! borrow and consume rules as ordinary functions.
